et_debt_ebitda":3.355445988346033,"_gp_net_debt_ebitda":false,"_score_net_debt_ebitda":14.3,"_gate_accruals":-0.06475128644939966,"_gp_accruals":true,"_score_accruals":57.7,"_gate_rev_volatility":0.13472174452843946,"_gp_rev_volatility":false,"_score_rev_volatility":75.8,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":0.020757478769950266,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":true,"_score_margin_vs_hist":74.1,"_gate_piotroski":6,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":66.7,"_gate_spread":-0.050669674905137935,"_gp_spread":false,"_score_spread":0.0,"_gate_roic_consistency":0.9644844019356681,"_gp_roic_consistency":false,"_score_roic_consistency":0.0,"_gate_incr_roic":-0.44108180934574814,"_gp_incr_roic":false,"_score_incr_roic":0.0,"_gate_margin_advantage":-0.006064149289871007,"_gp_margin_advantage":false,"_score_margin_advantage":31.3,"_gate_pool_share":-0.03144256787546518,"_gp_pool_share":false,"_score_pool_share":27.4,"_gate_rev_durability":0.014600002464417283,"_gp_rev_durability":false,"_score_rev_durability":32.3,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":0.0,"_gate_margins":-0.001681847097949024,"_gp_margins":false,"_score_margins":48.3,"_gate_fund_growth":0,"_gp_fund_growth":false,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.1,"_gate_share_shrink":0.04371573510570248,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.008741923223109084,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":85.4,"_gate_insider_own":0.23226,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Compass Minerals International, Inc. provides essential minerals in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, and internationally. It operates in two segments, Salt and Plant Nutrition. The Salt segment produces, markets, and sells sodium chloride and magnesium chloride, including rock salt, mechanically and solar evaporated salt, and brine and flake magnesium chloride products; and purchases and sells potassium chloride and calcium chloride to sell as finished products or to blend with sodium chloride to produce specialty products. This segment provides products for use as a deicer for roadways, consumer, and professional use; as an ingredient in chemical production; for water treatment, human, and animal nutrition; and for various other consumer and industrial uses, as well as records management services. The Plant Nutrition segment produces sulfate of potash specialty fertilizers in various grades that are used in broadcast spreaders, direct application, and liquid fertilizer solutions under the Protassium+ brand name; turf products used by the turf and ornamental markets, as well as for blends used on golf course greens; and organic products. This segment provides its products to distributors and retailers of crop inputs, as well as growers. The company was formerly known as Salt Holdings Corporation and changed its name to Compass Minerals International, Inc. in December 2003. Compass Minerals International, Inc. was founded in 1844 and is headquartered in Overland Park, Kansas.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Edward C. Dowling Jr., B.Sc., M.Sc., Ph.D. | President, CEO & Director | Age 70 | Compensation: $2.8M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $692K ranks in the above the sector median among Basic Materials peers. Each employee generates $9K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $6K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (9/10).","financial_summary":["Compass Minerals Intl Inc is a small-cap basic materials business with a $977M market cap on $1.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years and +1% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, and 23% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $17M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 1 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 6.18\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and thin 1.8\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 23% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 5.8% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Weakness in commodity prices pressures materials companies","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Commodity price volatility and cost-curve position \u2014 in a downturn only the lowest-cost producer has a business, and the rest carry the overhead.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Environmental remediation liability \u2014 tightening regulations on emissions, tailings, and water use create open-ended balance-sheet risk that grows with each legislative cycle.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Trade policy and tariff whiplash \u2014 materials producers are first-order casualties of protectionist swings, and margin gets squeezed from both input and output pricing.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk","Basic Materials sector outperforming the broader market over the past quarter"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Trading Systems Reacting to (CMP) Volatility","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/35/Trading_Systems_Reacting_to_CMP_Volatility_112626070802_1795694882.html","date":"2026-11-26","timestamp":1795694882.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Compass Minerals CEO Edward Dowling Settles PSUs at 167% of Target with Partial RSU Vesting","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/compass-minerals-ceo-edward-dowling-settles-psus-at-167-of-target-with-partial-rsu-vesting","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795805879.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":2,"scope":"company","tags":["leadership"]},{"title":"Compass Minerals' CCO Benjamin Nichols Vests 5,859 RSUs on November 24, 2026","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/compass-minerals-cco-benjamin-nichols-vests-5859-rsus-on-november-24-2026","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795805877.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":2,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Compass Minerals CFO Peter Fjellman Vesting 8,348 RSUs and Tax Withholding on November 24, 2026","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/compass-minerals-cfo-peter-fjellman-vesting-8348-rsus-and-tax-withholding-on-november-24-2026","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795805859.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":2,"scope":"company","tags":["leadership"]